isp: t41: fall back to i2c adapter 0 when the requested one is absent

The i2c adapter the ISP binds the sensor to is chosen in userspace and
passed down in the sensor info struct; nothing in the device tree or the
driver sets it. T40 boards put the sensor on adapter 1 and T41 boards on
adapter 0 - ipctool's own ingenic_open_i2c_fd() encodes exactly that
split - so a userspace stack built for one family asks the other for a
bus it does not have:

    subdev_sensor_ops_register_sensor: Failed to get I2C adapter 1, deferring probe!!!
    IMP_ISP_AddSensor failed [gc5603] / imp_system_init() failed

Renumbering the bus in the device tree looks like the obvious answer and
is a trap: drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ingenic.c derives its clock gate name
from the adapter id ("gate_i2c%d"), so a controller renumbered to 1 asks
for gate_i2c1 while its gate is gate_i2c0, comes up unclocked, and every
transfer ends in "--I2C pio write wait timeout".

Handle it where the number is used instead: if the requested adapter does
not exist, fall back to the first one and say so. A board that really has
the requested bus is unaffected.

Note the firmware blob carries a weak definition of this wrapper, so the
strong one here is the one linked in.

Signed-off-by: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>

diff --git a/kernel/isp/t41/tx-isp-funcs.c b/kernel/isp/t41/tx-isp-funcs.c
index 7c08586..6a45b9b 100644
--- a/kernel/isp/t41/tx-isp-funcs.c
+++ b/kernel/isp/t41/tx-isp-funcs.c
@@ -508,7 +508,30 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(private_clk_set_rate);
 /* i2c interfaces */
 struct i2c_adapter* private_i2c_get_adapter(int nr)
 {
-	return i2c_get_adapter(nr);
+	struct i2c_adapter *adap = i2c_get_adapter(nr);
+
+	/*
+	 * The adapter number is chosen in userspace and handed to the ISP in
+	 * the sensor info struct. T40 boards put the sensor on adapter 1 and
+	 * T41 boards on adapter 0, so a userspace stack built for one family
+	 * asks for a bus the other does not have, and sensor registration
+	 * fails with "Failed to get I2C adapter N, deferring probe".
+	 *
+	 * Renumbering the bus in the device tree is not an option: the Ingenic
+	 * i2c driver derives its clock gate name from the adapter id
+	 * ("gate_i2c%d"), so a renumbered controller ends up enabling the wrong
+	 * gate and every transfer times out.
+	 *
+	 * If the requested adapter simply is not there, fall back to the first
+	 * one. On a board that has the requested bus nothing changes.
+	 */
+	if (!adap && nr != 0) {
+		adap = i2c_get_adapter(0);
+		if (adap)
+			ISP_WARNING("i2c adapter %d absent, using adapter 0\n", nr);
+	}
+
+	return adap;
 }
 
 void private_i2c_put_adapter(struct i2c_adapter *adap)
